{
  "slug": "partitivos",
  "number": 6,
  "part": 2,
  "title": "Du, de la, de l', des",
  "tagline": "Partitive articles: food and quantities, without mixing up likes and eating.",
  "goal": "Order food, talk about what you eat and drink, write a shopping list and tell «I like chocolate» apart from «I eat chocolate».",
  "minutes": 9,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "que-es",
      "title": "What a partitive is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The partitive expresses an **unspecified amount** of something: some of it, not all of it. In English we often say **nothing** («I drink water», «I eat bread») or «some»; in French you **always need an article**."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je bois de l'eau.",
              "tr": "I drink water."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Je mange du pain.",
              "tr": "I eat bread."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Je veux des fraises.",
              "tr": "I want (some) strawberries."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "formas",
      "title": "The four forms",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "When",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*du*",
              "Masculine singular",
              "*du pain, du fromage, du lait*"
            ],
            [
              "*de la*",
              "Feminine singular",
              "*de la confiture, de la soupe, de la viande*"
            ],
            [
              "*de l'*",
              "Singular starting with a vowel or silent h",
              "*de l'eau, de l'huile, de l'ananas*"
            ],
            [
              "*des*",
              "Plural",
              "*des fraises, des œufs, des légumes*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*du* is the contraction of *de + le*. You never say «de le»."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "Boire (to drink), irregular",
          "text": "*je bois, tu bois, il boit, nous buvons, vous buvez, ils boivent*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gusto-consumo",
      "title": "Liking or eating",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "When you talk about…",
            "Use",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**Likes**: *aimer, adorer, détester, préférer*",
              "*le, la, l', les*",
              "*J'adore {le} chocolat.* (chocolate in general)"
            ],
            [
              "**Consuming**: *manger, boire, acheter, vouloir, prendre*",
              "*du, de la, de l', des*",
              "*Je mange {du} chocolat.* (some chocolate)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "If you could say «some» in English, use the partitive. If you mean the thing in general, use *le, la, les*: English says «I love chocolate» without «the», French says *J'adore le chocolat*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "negativo",
      "title": "In the negative, everything becomes de",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "After *ne… pas*, partitives (and *un, une*) become **de**, or **d'** before a vowel, like English «not any»:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je ne mange pas de viande.",
              "tr": "I don't eat meat.",
              "note": "de la → de"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il ne boit pas de café.",
              "tr": "He doesn't drink coffee.",
              "note": "du → de"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Je n'ai pas d'argent.",
              "tr": "I don't have any money.",
              "note": "de l' → d'"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Exception: with *être* it doesn't change. *C'est du thé ?* → *Non, ce n'est pas du thé, c'est du café.*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cantidades",
      "title": "After quantities, also de",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Quantity",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*beaucoup de* (a lot of)",
              "*beaucoup de sucre*"
            ],
            [
              "*un peu de* (a little)",
              "*un peu de lait*"
            ],
            [
              "*trop de* (too much)",
              "*trop de sel*"
            ],
            [
              "*assez de* (enough)",
              "*assez d'eau*"
            ],
            [
              "*un kilo de*",
              "*un kilo de tomates*"
            ],
            [
              "*une bouteille de*",
              "*une bouteille d'eau*"
            ],
            [
              "*un verre de*",
              "*un verre de vin*"
            ],
            [
              "*une tranche de*",
              "*une tranche de jambon*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To replace all of this with a pronoun (*j'en veux*, I want some), see [[16]]."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
